This is a modest restructuring and I expect it to be at best marginal. I mask S1 and S2 first and shift the combined pair once, which changes the order of operations but not the number of reductions. I did not find a way to share one reduction across the three columns. Because the reduction fills every slot, the columns can only be separated by masks applied after it.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));

  vector<Ciphertext> s;
  for (const auto &column : columns)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * column;
    for (size_t steps = slots() >> 1; steps > 0; steps >>= 1)
      r += r << static_cast<int>(steps);
    s.push_back(r);
  }

  Ciphertext m1 = s[1] * first_slot;
  Ciphertext m2 = s[2] * first_slot;
  Ciphertext tail = m1 + (m2 >> 1);
  Ciphertext output = s[0] * first_slot + (tail >> 1);
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I could not find a circuit with fewer operations. The columns can only be separated by a mask after the reduction, and `first_slot` is the only mask available. This variant reduces in ascending shift order and masks before combining, so it is another reordering of the same work and is unlikely to beat the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));

  vector<Ciphertext> m;
  for (const auto &column : columns)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * column;
    for (size_t steps = 1; steps < slots(); steps <<= 1)
      r += r << static_cast<int>(steps);
    m.push_back(r * first_slot);
  }

  Ciphertext output = m[0] + ((m[1] + (m[2] >> 1)) >> 1);
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I found no circuit with fewer operations. Separating the columns needs one mask per column, and only `first_slot` is available. This variant is not expected to win: it reduces `t_0` fully, then reduces `t_1` and `t_2` together, adding the shifted pair to the already reduced `t_0` before the mask.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  Plaintext t0("table_0"), t1("table_1"), t2("table_2");

  Ciphertext a = indicator * t0;
  Ciphertext b = indicator * t1;
  Ciphertext c = indicator * t2;
  for (size_t s = slots() >> 1; s > 0; s >>= 1)
  {
    a += a << static_cast<int>(s);
    b += b << static_cast<int>(s);
    c += c << static_cast<int>(s);
  }
  Ciphertext output = a * first_slot;
  output += (b * first_slot) >> 1;
  output += (c * first_slot) >> 2;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I have no approach that cuts rotations. This variant is a different circuit: it reduces the nested sums t0, t0+t1 and t0+t1+t2 and recovers the individual columns by subtraction after the reduction. It should cost about the same as the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  Plaintext first_slot("first_slot");
  Plaintext p0("table_0"), p1("table_1"), p2("table_2");

  Ciphertext t0 = indicator * p0;
  Ciphertext t1 = indicator * p1;
  Ciphertext t2 = indicator * p2;
  Ciphertext a = t0;
  Ciphertext b = t0 + t1;
  Ciphertext c = b + t2;
  for (size_t s = slots() >> 1; s > 0; s >>= 1)
  {
    a += a << static_cast<int>(s);
    b += b << static_cast<int>(s);
    c += c << static_cast<int>(s);
  }
  // a = S0, b = S0+S1, c = S0+S1+S2 (in every slot)
  Ciphertext d1 = b - a;
  Ciphertext d2 = c - b;
  Ciphertext output = a * first_slot;
  output += (d1 * first_slot) >> 1;
  output += (d2 * first_slot) >> 2;
  output.set_output("output");
}
```

I could not find a cheaper circuit. Any single-reduction packing needs slot masks other than `first_slot`, or constants such as 1/2 to unmix the columns, and neither is available. I am resubmitting the baseline structure, which is the best valid kernel I have.

